Charnel-Skank
Ingredients: (ROOTS) REGGAE + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Oboe
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from a desert caravan culture that trades in salvaged synthesizers.

Charnel-Skank is a sepulchral, sun-scorched clash of heavy, half-time roots reggae one-drop rhythms and chilling gothic deathrock gloom, pierced by the snake-charming wail of an overdriven oboe and drowned in the unstable, heat-damaged filters of scavenged analog synthesizers.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 62–72 BPM. A lurching, deliberate half-time one-drop pulse where the cavernous kick and rimshot land heavily on beat three, counterbalanced by dragged off-beat chops that imitate the slow, rhythmic stride of pack beasts through dunes.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor keys steeped in Aeolian dread, Phrygian Dominant, and Arabic Hijaz maqams; flat-second tensions, unresolved suspended fourths, and suffocating minor-ninth chord clusters.
- Production Signatures: (1) Sand-Damaged Bucket-Brigade Delay, where analog repeats degrade into noisy, gritty flutters; (2) Cistern Reverb, captured by re-amping signals through empty, subterranean desert water tombs; (3) Direct-Injected Double-Reed Saturation, running the acoustic oboe through germanium fuzz pedals and tape saturators until its nasal resonance buzzes like an angry mechanical hornet.
- Dynamics: Glacial, suspenseful builds that crawl out of desiccated synth drones and lonesome oboe laments, violently shattered by earth-splitting sub-bass drops on the downbeat, cycling through hypnotic dubwise dropouts and sudden, terrifying walls of cavernous feedback.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the shifting dunes of Erg-al-Maut, Charnel-Skank was conjured by the Sulkha, a nomadic brotherhood of scavengers who unearth pre-collapse broadcast telemetry stations and buried flight decks to harvest operational circuit boards and synthesizer modules. Swathed in indigo burial shrouds with face veils stitched from copper ground wires, they haul their precious electrical salvage upon dromedary litters, running patch cables directly into roaring two-stroke gasoline generators. When the desert caravans encamp beneath freezing, starless skies, they align their sound systems in outward-facing semicircles, using their subsonic roots basslines to ward off predatory desert factions and invoke ancestral grief. Listeners gather around smoldering frankincense pots and burning circuit solder, swaying backward in a slow, catatonic "coffin-rock" while the oboe's microtonal cries mimic the death rattles of long-drowned civilizations.

Typical Instruments:
- Ebony and Brass Oboes: Modified with throat-contact transducers and fed through analog spring reverbs and fuzz boxes.
- Salvaged Monophonic Synthesizers: Battered analog synths (salvaged Korg, Moog, and Soviet Polivoks clones) producing detuned sub-bass and drifting drone pads.
- Roots Drum Array: A dampened, deep-tuned mahogany kick drum, a crackling brass snare with wires disengaged for bone-dry rimshots, and tuned copper water pipes played as dub timbales.
- Gothic "Coffin" Bass: Fretless electric bass played with felt-covered picks, run through octave-dividers for subterranean low-end presence.
- Barbed Skank Guitar: Electric guitars played through vintage chorus and plate reverb units, providing icy, brittle chops on the off-beats.

